{"i": 406, "gt": "syringoma", "d": "Acne Keloidalis Nuchae (or potentially Folliculitis), but given the location on the forehead/scalp, it's more likely to be Acne Vulgaris or Folliculitis Barbae if it were beard area, but here it looks like follicular hyperkeratosis or acne. Let's look closer. The image shows a forehead with numerous small, firm papules and some larger nodules/cysts. There is significant scarring and textural change. This pattern is classic for severe inflammatory acne vulgaris, specifically nodulocystic acne, which has led to atrophic and hypertrophic scarring. Another possibility is dissecting cellulitis of the scalp, but that usually presents as boggy nodules draining sinus tracts, often on the back of the head/occiput. This is on the frontal hairline. It could also be a form of folliculitis decalvans, but that usually causes patchy alopecia. Given the dense clustering of pustules/papules and scarring on the forehead/hairline, severe acne vulgaris is the strongest candidate. Another differential is keratosis pilaris, but that doesn't cause this level of inflammation and scarring. Hidradenitis suppurativa? No, wrong location. So, Acne Vulgaris (Nodulocystic) is #1. Folliculitis is #2. Acne Keloidalis Nuchae is #3 (though usually nape). Discoid Lupus Erythematosus is #4 (can cause scarring alopecia and plaques, but the lesions look more cystic/pustular). Rosacea? Unlikely to have this much scarring. Seborrheic dermatitis? No, that's scaling.", "t6": ["Acne Vulgaris (Nodulocystic)", "Folliculitis", "Acne Keloidalis Nuchae", "Discoid Lupus Erythematosus", "Hidradenitis Suppurativa", "Keratosis Pilaris"]}
